# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Skip and the consequence rule" (first part).
id: skip-consequence
title: Skip and the consequence rule
topic: Skip and consequence
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Skip and the consequence rule"
tags: skip, consequence, implication

task:
  Using the Skip rule and the consequence rule, show that the following
  triple is valid.

pre: x > 5
post: x > 0

program:
  {P}
  {P'}
  skip
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Apply the Skip rule to the postcondition
rule: SKIP
focus: skip
text:
  The Skip rule only produces triples whose precondition and
  postcondition are the same formula. We apply it backwards, starting from
  the postcondition $x > 0$. It gives the precondition $P'$ below.
calc:
  wp(skip, Q)
  = x > 0
set: P' = x > 0
instance: SKIP
  ---
  {x > 0} skip {x > 0}

step: Link the precondition with the consequence rule
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  The precondition of the specification, $x > 5$, is not the formula that
  the Skip rule gave us. The consequence rule accepts it if it implies
  that formula.
vc: x > 5 ==> x > 0
why:
  Every integer greater than $5$ is greater than $0$. In terms of sets of
  states, $\{6, 7, 8, ...\}$ is included in $\{1, 2, 3, ...\}$, so the
  precondition is stronger than the formula given by the Skip rule.
instance: CONSEQUENCE
  x > 5 ==> x > 0
  {x > 0} skip {x > 0}
  ---
  {x > 5} skip {x > 0}
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The Skip rule alone cannot prove this triple, because its two
  assertions differ. The consequence rule bridges the difference with the
  implication $x > 5 ==> x > 0$. In general, $\{P\}$ `skip` $\{Q\}$ is
  valid exactly when $P ==> Q$.
